diff --git a/Documentation/userspace-api/uts-identity.rst b/Documentation/userspace-api/uts-identity.rst
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..c33dbbd6a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/userspace-api/uts-identity.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,106 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+Host-managed UTS kernel identity
+================================
+
+This project extension is enabled by CONFIG_UTS_NS_IDENTITY. It configures a
+noninitial UTS namespace through an open namespace file descriptor, without
+changing the kernel implementation or the architecture reported to programs.
+Unconfigured namespaces keep their existing behavior.
+
+Interface
+---------
+
+``NS_UTS_GET_IDENTITY`` and ``NS_UTS_SET_IDENTITY`` are defined in
+``<linux/nsfs.h>``. Both operate on ``struct uts_ns_identity``, a fixed 144-byte
+structure with the same layout in native and compat ABIs::
+
+    __u32 abi_version;
+    __u32 flags;
+    char release[65];
+    char version[65];
+    __u8 reserved[6];
+
+GET writes ABI version 1, the current release and version, and zero reserved
+bytes. ``UTS_NS_IDENTITY_SEALED`` is set in flags after configuration. GET does
+not require an input structure. The presence of GET and its returned version
+provide capability detection; a kernel without this extension returns ENOTTY.
+
+SET requires ABI version 1, zero flags and zero reserved bytes. Strings must
+contain 1 to 64 printable ASCII characters followed by a NUL. Release must not
+contain spaces. Trailing bytes after the NUL are not stored. The caller needs
+CAP_SYS_ADMIN in the initial user namespace, must belong to that user namespace,
+and must have the initial active PID namespace. The initial UTS namespace cannot
+be configured. The capability check is performed against the ioctl caller,
+rather than the credentials of the process that opened the namespace FD.
+
+The first successful SET atomically installs both strings and seals the
+namespace. Later SET requests, including those from the administrator, return
+EBUSY. There is no reset operation. A fresh instance requires a fresh UTS
+namespace. Invalid ABI versions, flags, reserved bytes and strings return
+EINVAL; invalid user pointers return EFAULT. Both ioctls return EINVAL on a
+namespace FD of another type. Unauthorized operations return EPERM.
+
+Configure the namespace before starting guest code or creating its descendant
+UTS namespaces. A descendant created before SET has its own unconfigured copy
+and is not retroactively configured. These ioctls do not notify sysctl pollers;
+release and version are initialized before the guest begins executing.
+
+Inheritance and access
+----------------------
+
+A namespace created with CLONE_NEWUTS inherits the parent's strings, sealed
+state and identity family. The identity family is the boot-unique namespace
+ID of the namespace on which SET succeeded. Independent SET operations create
+different families even when all their strings are identical.
+
+A task in a sealed family may use GET and setns within that family. It may not
+use either operation on an unconfigured UTS namespace, the initial namespace,
+or another family, even if it possesses an open FD and the usual namespace
+capabilities. Host administrators meeting the SET credential requirements may
+inspect and switch between families. Existing setns capability checks still
+apply. Unconfigured tasks retain standard switching rules between unconfigured
+UTS namespaces; entering a sealed family requires a host administrator.
+
+This extension does not make possession of initial user/PID namespace root
+credentials safe for guest code. The runtime must provide a separate PID
+namespace, restrict access to host tasks and files, and apply its normal device,
+mount, capability, seccomp and LSM policy. It must not hand host namespace FDs
+to the guest as a substitute for those controls.
+
+Views and compatibility
+-----------------------
+
+uname, including raw native and compat syscalls, and the osrelease/version
+sysctls read the configured UTS strings. Sysname and machine are unchanged;
+normal personality-dependent architecture handling remains in effect. The full
+release/version arrays returned by uname are zero-padded so bytes from the old
+host identity do not survive after a shorter configured string.
+
+For a configured namespace, /proc/version is rendered as::
+
+    <sysname> version <release> <version>\n
+
+It contains no additional compile-time user, hostname or compiler string. The
+host and unconfigured namespaces retain their original banner. Kernel log
+banners and other global diagnostic files are outside this interface and need
+separate runtime access controls.
+
+UNAME26 compatibility maps the configured release using its minor version and
+suffix, instead of the build release and LINUX_VERSION_PATCHLEVEL. A 3.x release
+uses 40 + minor; other numeric releases use 60 + minor. A missing or overflowing
+minor leaves the configured release unchanged. It never falls back to host
+identity. Old uname entry points use the same mapping where the architecture
+implements them. On arm64, AArch32 uses the compat newuname entry point; the
+removed ARM old-uname syscall numbers still return ENOSYS.
+
+Validation
+----------
+
+The filesystems/nsfs ``uts_identity`` selftest requires an initial-namespace
+administrator and exercises ABI validation, one-time sealing, libc/raw uname,
+sysctl and banner agreement, UNAME26, inherited namespaces, same-family setns,
+FD-based escape attempts, nested user namespaces and unchanged host identity.
+It accepts an optional path to a compat executable, run inside the configured
+guest. The project validation fixture uses a freestanding AArch32 executable
+for raw uname, ioctl and personality checks on arm64.
